e file imports `node:assert/strict`, so `equal` is strict anyway. No issue.\n\nNo security, quality, or correctness issues found. The diff is a minimal, well-tested scaffold that exactly matches the story spec.\n\n## Review Summary\n\n| Severity | Count | Status |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| CRITICAL | 0     | pass   |\n| HIGH     | 0     | pass   |\n| MEDIUM   | 0     | info   |\n| LOW      | 0     | note   |\n\nVerdict: APPROVE\n</output>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a43cfa15-6a09-4e2c-a980-4ebf962d7f30","uuid":"074310e8-d4e2-420f-b0cd-bc4f4d5dc0f0","timestamp":"2026-08-20T15:39:23.854Z","tool_use_result":{"retrieval_status":"success","task":{"task_id":"a93232fdeb027137d","task_type":"local_agent","status":"completed","description":"code-reviewer: final review of STORY-setup-001a","prompt":"You are a senior code reviewer ensuring high standards of code quality and security.\n\n## Review Process\n\nWhen invoked:\n\n1. **Gather context** — Run `git diff --staged` and `git diff` to see all changes. If no diff, check recent commits with `git log --oneline -5`.\n2. **Understand scope** — Identify which files changed, what feature/fix they relate to, and how they connect.\n3. **Read surrounding code** — Don't review changes in isolation. Read the full file and understand imports, dependencies, and call sites.\n4. **Apply review checklist** — Work through each category below, from CRITICAL to LOW.\n5. **Report findings** — Use the output format below. Only report issues you are confident about (>80% sure it is a real problem).\n\n## Confidence-Based Filtering\n\n**IMPORTANT**: Do not flood the review with noise. Apply these filters:\n\n- **Report** if you are >80% confident it is a real issue\n- **Skip** stylistic preferences unless they violate project conventions\n- **Skip** issues in unchanged code unless they are CRITICAL security issues\n- **Consolidate** similar issues\n- **Prioritize** issues that could cause bugs, security vulnerabilities, or data loss\n\n### Pre-Report Gate\n\nBefore writing a finding, answer all four questions. If any answer is \"no\" or \"unsure\", downgrade severity or drop the finding.\n\n1. **Can I cite the exact line?** Name the file and line. Vague findings must be dropped.\n2. **Can I describe the concrete failure mode?** Name the input, state, and bad outcome.\n3. **Have I read the surrounding context?** Check callers, imports, and tests.\n4. **Is the severity defensible?** Severity inflation erodes trust faster than missed findings.\n\n### HIGH / CRITICAL Require Proof\n\nFor any finding tagged HIGH or CRITICAL, include:\n- The exact snippet and line number\n- The specific failure scenario: input, state, and outcome\n- Why existing guards do not catch it\n\nIf you cannot produce all three, demote to MEDIUM or drop.\n\n### It Is Acceptable And Expected To Return Zero Findings\n\nA clean review is a valid review. Do not manufacture findings. If the diff is small, well-tested, and follows the project's patterns, the correct output is a summary with zero rows and verdict `APPROVE`.\n\n## Common False Positives - Skip These\n\nSkip unless you have evidence specific to this codebase: \"consider adding error handling\" on handled paths, \"missing input validation\" for internal functions whose callers validate, \"magic number\" for well-known constants, \"function too long\" for config objects or test tables, \"missing JSDoc\" on self-describing helpers, \"prefer const over let\" when reassigned, \"possible null dereference\" when type is narrowed, \"N+1 query\" on fixed-cardinality loops, \"missing await\" on intentional fire-and-forget, \"should use TypeScript\" in a JS-only project, \"hardcoded value\" in test fixtures, security theater.\n\n## Review Checklist\n\n### Security (CRITICAL)\nHardcoded credentials, SQL injection, XSS, path traversal, CSRF, authentication bypasses, insecure dependencies, exposed secrets in logs.\n\n### Code Quality (HIGH)\nLarge functions (>50 lines), large files (>800 lines), deep nesting (>4 levels), missing error handling, mutation patterns, console.log statements, missing tests, dead code.\n\n### Node.js/Backend Patterns (HIGH)\nUnvalidated input, missing rate limiting, unbounded queries, N+1 queries, missing timeouts, error message leakage.\n\n### Performance (MEDIUM)\nInefficient algorithms, blocking I/O in async contexts.\n\n### Best Practices (LOW)\nTODO/FIXME without tickets, poor naming, inconsistent formatting.\n\n## Review Output Format\n\nOrganize findings by severity with File/Issue/Fix for each. End with:\n\n```\n## Review Summary\n\n| Severity | Count | Status |\n|----------|-------|--------|\n| CRITICAL | n     | pass   |\n| HIGH     | n     | warn   |\n| MEDIUM   | n     | info   |\n| LOW      | n     | note   |\n\nVerdict: APPROVE / WARNING / BLOCK\n```\n\n### Approval Criteria\n- **Approve**: No CRITICAL or HIGH issues, including clean reviews with zero findings.\n- **Warning**: HIGH issues only (can merge with caution)\n- **Block**: CRITICAL issues found — must fix before merge\n\nDo not withhold approval to appear rigorous. If the diff is clean, approve it.\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nReview the work for story **STORY-setup-001a** in the workspace at\n`/home/nitin-sharma/ralph-workspaces/6896163a-932b-4b96-bfe9-cdbb022c3538/c7df6267-784a-460a-addd-6d781a21b6f5`.\n\nAlways `cd` to that workspace root first before running any command.\n\nThe story: \"Create Root NextMove package.json With Workspaces\" — root `package.json` for the NextMove monorepo declaring npm workspaces over `apps/*`, pinned npm and Node 22.x, turbo 2.x devDependency, and turbo run scripts.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n1. Root package.json exists with name nextmove, private true, workspaces array containing apps/*, packageManager pinned to npm@10, and engines.node 22.x.\n2. devDependencies pin turbo 2.x and scripts define build, test, and lint as turbo run build / turbo run test / turbo run lint.\n3. The scaffold files are commit